<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seven Interpretive Essays on Peruvian Reality</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Siete ensayos de interpretación de la realidad peruana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seven Interpretive Essays on Peruvian Reality (Siete ensayos de interpretación de la realidad peruana) is a book by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-carlos-mariategui">José Carlos Mariátegui</a> published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in 1928. Applying Marxist analysis to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, it treated the condition of the indigenous population as a problem of land and examined the economy, education, religion, regionalism and literature. It became one of the most influential works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> social thought.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-carlos-mariategui">José Carlos Mariátegui</a> built the book from articles he had published in magazines such as Mundial and Amauta, proposing to apply historical materialism to a full re-evaluation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. In the prologue he warned that he was not an impartial critic and that his judgements were shaped by his ideals and passions. He planned an eighth essay on Peru's political and ideological evolution but kept it for a separate book, and he called none of the essays finished; his death two years later ended these plans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The first essay traces <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s economy. Mariátegui described the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> economy as socialist and collectivist, resting on collective ownership of land, water and pasture and common labour, and argued that the Spanish conquest replaced it with a feudal order based on mining and, on the coast, on imported black slaves. Under the Republic this feudalism survived as gamonalismo, the domination of indigenous communities by landowners of European descent, beside a weak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> that arose in the age of guano and nitrate.</p><p>Hence the problem of the Indian was, in his words, &quot;more than pedagogical&quot; an economic and social one: no law could protect indigenous people while the latifundio and servitude survived, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> class that followed the Spanish had kept the colonial structures. The essay on education held that a country's teaching could not be democratised without democratising its economy, and welcomed the university reform movement begun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, in 1918. The essay on religion described the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> faith as a moral code serving the state, and the essay on regionalism argued that federalism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> had been a demand of the gamonales rather than of the people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The book has been reissued dozens of times and translated into Russian, French, English, Italian, Portuguese and Hungarian. In the late 1960s, at a conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abimael-guzman">Abimael Guzmán</a>, later leader of the Peruvian Communist Party, called it &quot;an unshakable document&quot;, and the historian Marc Becker has praised its Marxist analysis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> problems. Víctor Andrés Belaunde, a Catholic thinker, answered it with La Realidad Nacional, listing what he saw as its errors and omissions, while Juan Carlos Grijalva has argued that Mariátegui idealised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> past.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seven Interpretive Essays on Peruvian Reality” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…such as Ricardo Palma, author of the Tradiciones peruanas, shaped a national memory, and in the twentieth century José Carlos Mariátegui’s <b>Seven Interpretive Essays on Peruvian Reality</b> made Peru a centre of Latin American Marxist thought, while Haya de la Torre’s APRA inspired populist parties across the continent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
